NAME
    mkdisk - make a new disk on the second drive

SYNOPSIS
    mkdisk [-f] [kernel]

DESCRIPTION
    Writes a complete new disk on drive 1: the boot block, the kernel
    (/lib/y1os, or the file named), an empty volume, then every file and
    directory of this disk, each directory keeping its size and each
    file its load and exec address. Deleted files are not copied, so the
    new disk needs no pack; neither are a build's work files, make.sh
    and ccw.*. It prints each directory as it starts, and a summary:

        mkdisk: 18 sectors of kernel, 231 files, 14 directories, ...

    mkdisk is /bin/mkdisk. Drive 1 is the IDE slave beside the running
    disk (on the emulators, the image of -C). The running disk is only
    read. The boot block goes last: until then drive 1 has no volume.

    -f writes over a drive that already holds a volume.

    To rebuild the system onto a new disk:

        cd /src
        make               every program, then the kernel
        make install       into /bin, /lib/cc and /lib/y1os
        make clean         the build's output leaves /src
        cd /
        mkdisk

    To boot the new disk it must become drive 0, the only one the ROM's
    O boots: swap the cards, or the images on an emulator.

LIMITS
    One disk's files onto one drive; no drive 2. The name of a file is
    not changed. 62 characters of path, as everywhere. kermit does not
    build on the machine: mkdisk copies the /bin/kermit that is there.
    Messages: "mkdisk: no drive 1", "mkdisk: drive 1 holds a volume
    (-f writes over it)", "mkdisk: no kernel", "mkdisk: drive 1 does
    not answer". Each ends with status 1.

